WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:06.660
Created by cartoon veteran Loren Bouchard,
Bob's Burgers mixes silly stories, puns, heart,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.820
and music to make for a truly original and
wholesome viewing experience.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.780
Get ready to dive between the buns, because
we're taking a look at the untold truth of

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.020
Bob's Burgers.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.140
The way a TV show gets made is pretty straightforward...and
difficult.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.400
A writer comes up with an idea for a show,
writes a pilot script, and, if they've got

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.640
enough credits and clout in Hollywood, they've
got an agent, and that agent gets the pilot

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.160
into the hands of the executives at TV networks
who decide what shows they might want to make.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.300
This is not how Bob's Burgers originated.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.340
Creator Loren Bouchard was actually approached
by Suzanna Makkos, the executive vice president

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.740
in charge of comedy at Fox, and asked to create
an animated series for the network.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.720
Makkos was working on developing another animated
series and was looking at demo reels by animation

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:50.220
houses.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.539
One of them included some clips of Lucy, the
Daughter of the Devil, a macabre comedy that

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.080
had briefly run on Adult Swim.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.640
Makkos liked the animation fine, but what
she really liked was the show.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.500
She thought it was funny and had an assistant
track down its creator, Loren Bouchard.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.850
He was living in San Francisco at the time
and flew down to meet with Makkos about making

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.080
a show that same day.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.039
Because cartoons are so expensive and complicated
to produce, some will forgo making a complete

00:01:15.039 --> 00:01:19.770
30-minute pilot in favor of a "pilot presentation,"
a short, roughly animated film of just a few

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.880
minutes that gives network executives a good
idea of what a completed series would feel

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:24.380
like.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.960
Bouchard and producer Jim Dauterive made one
such pilot presentation for Fox.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.240
Fox entertainment head Kevin Reilly liked
the overall concept and feel of the show,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.080
but he had some notes.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.930
Among them were that the three kids' personalities
were not unique enough from one another.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:42.960
At that time, the Belcher kids consisted of
one girl, Louise, and two boys, Gene and Daniel.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.000
While Bouchard had to admit that Reilly was
correct, neither he nor Fox wanted to let

00:01:47.009 --> 00:01:49.000
go of Dan Mintz, who played Daniel.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.520
The solution: change Daniel to a girl named
Tina, keep Mintz in the part, and don't have

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.180
him change his voice or approach to the character
at all.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.060
"I'm no hero.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.800
I put my bra on one boob at a time, just like
everyone else."

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.360
The last thing that Bouchard had worked on
before Bob's Burgers was Lucy, Daughter of

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.600
the Devil for Adult Swim.

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.599
That show was about the battle between good
and evil, and so at the time, Bouchard had

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:12.569
trained himself to skew his comedy on the
more twisted side.

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:16.730
That led to a very dark initial premise for
Bob's Burgers: The Belchers were going to

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.300
run a burger joint...but they were cannibals.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:19.800
Bouchard explained:

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.660
"I had this kind of more occult-y, sort of
darker edge to the way I was thinking then."

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.790
Ultimately, Fox loved everything about the
show except the cannibalism.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.840
Suzanna Makkos talked Bouchard out of it,
pointing out how difficult it would be to

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.819
do, quote, "100 episodes' worth of cannibal
jokes."

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:38.739
Elements of the original concept remains:
Mort's Funeral Home next door was possibly

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:41.010
going to be a meat supplier for Bob's Burgers.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.040
The very first episode even pays homage to
the original premise!

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.700
"Why'd you get run out of Bog Harbor?"

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.780
"For selling human flesh to the restaurants."

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.480
"Haha, I'm kidding, people!

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:50.980
Come on!"

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.060
The voice cast of Bob's Burgers was initially
populated by veteran professional comedians

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.060
and voice actors.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.579
But that's not the case for the actor who
landed the role of boisterous, prone-to-singing

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:02.580
Linda Belcher.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.620
"I'm a Ta-Ta and I'm gonna rock ya!

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.400
I'm singing from the heart!

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.720
I still got two outta five, I still got two
outta five, I still got two out of five sexy

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:12.220
parts!

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:12.720
Woo!"

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.840
A lot of people start making YouTube videos
with visions of stardom.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.050
The dream has only come true so far for a
relative handful and John Roberts, who voices

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:20.960
Linda, is one of them.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:25.400
His videos, such as "The Christmas Tree" and
"The Phone Call," in which he played overbearing

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:30.100
older women, went viral and led to appearances
on a number of shows playing those kinds of

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.850
characters, such as on Late Night with Jimmy
Fallon, where he played the host's mother.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:38.340
That all led to a meeting with Loren Bouchard,
and a role on Bob's Burgers playing a character

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.100
much like the ones he created online.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.910
In 2015, Roberts became the first member of
the Bob's Burgers cast to earn an Emmy Award

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.660
nomination for outstanding character voice-over
performance.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.980
The show's cast consists primarily of major
players on the "alternative" comedy scene,

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:58.170
such as Kristen Schaal, Dan Mintz, and Eugene
Mirman, along with voice acting legend H.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:58.980
Jon Benjamin.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.560
Also part of the cast is Academy Award-winning
actor and theater star Kevin Kline.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:08.120
Bob's Burgers represents his first major voice
work for a weekly animated TV series.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.760
They got him to join the cast in a very non-traditional
way.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.140
Kline's grown children live in New York and
go to a lot of comedy shows, and are big fans

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.520
of Mirman in particular.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.320
They brought their father along to some shows,
and Kline and Mirman got along.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.580
As it turns out, Kline's manager was friends
with the Bob's Burgers casting director, so

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.320
the show went out on a limb and asked him
if he'd be interested in the recurring role

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.260
of Bob's eccentric millionaire landlord, Mr.
Fischoeder.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.370
"The truth is, Bob, I was only raising the
rent so you would notice me."

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:35.960
"Really?"

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:36.460
"No.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.520
I wanted a pool.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.080
You can't get chicks with a hedge maze."

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.280
"Sure, makes sense."

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.590
Kline read the script, thought it was funny,
and accepted, to the apparent disbelief of

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:45.200
the crew.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.080
Bouchard admitted:

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.520
"I don't think any of us were prepared for
him to say yes."

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.020
The vast majority of animated TV series, past
and present, do not record their actors doing

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.380
their voice parts all at once.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.960
Rather, each individual actor goes into a
recording studio and delivers lines one by

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:00.460
one.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:04.920
Bob's Burgers is an exception audio for each
episode is collected from the entire cast

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:05.480
all at once.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.960
It's not quite a table read with microphones,
or like shooting a live-action sitcom some

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:14.791
of the actors live in Los Angeles, and some
live in New York, so a high-quality ISDN line

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.340
is used to unite everybody.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.440
But it is all done live, with the actors feeding
off each other.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.380
This gives the show a realistic, conversational
feel with plenty of room for improvisation

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:29.640
similar to Bouchard's previous natural-sounding
series, Home Movies and Dr. Katz: Professional

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.140
Therapist.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.520
Even big celebrities popping in for an episode
have to work this unique method of production

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:35.980
into their busy schedules.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.280
Denizens of the Internet have a number of
theories about why youngest Belcher child

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.220
Louise always wears bunny ears.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.310
It's never quite explained why; maybe she
has a bald spot she's hiding, or the hat allows

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.350
her to hide from the world in some small way.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.780
The real reason is not so emotionally complicated.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:55.040
It was inspired by a manga and later, anime
from Japan called Tekkonkinkreet.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.780
Show creator Loren Bouchard explained:

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.580
"There was a kid who had this little bear
hat, and I liked that weird combination of

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.600
kid and animal where they become an animal
by always being seen wearing these little

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:05.100
ears.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.120
You get a little cognitive dissonance where
you experience Louise both as a little girl

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.810
and Bugs Bunny at the same time."

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.960
That animal instinct is alive and well in
Louise, as are references to Japanese pop

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.480
culture in Bob's Burgers.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.800
One episode features an extended dream sequence/tribute
to Studio Ghibli's My Neighbor Totoro, the

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.370
similarly Ghibli-inspired episode that takes
place inside Louise's fever-riddled brain,

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:30.710
and Bob and Louise's love of a series of Japanese
martial arts films called Hawk &amp; Chick.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.460
"I know we're fighting this seaweed monster
right now, but I want you to know how much

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.440
I love and support you even if you decide,
hold on.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:38.940
Huh!

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:39.460
Hiya!"

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:43.160
Whether or not it has something to do with
one of those things, Bouchard mentioned on

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:47.970
Twitter in 2018 that the true origin of Louise's
bunny ears hat habit will be explained in

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.520
Bob's Burgers: The Movie, which hits theaters
in July 2020.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.600
One of the most popular recurring features
on Bob's Burgers is the daily burger special

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:57.600
at Bob's Burgers.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.740
Bob always finds a way to incorporate new
or special ingredients to make an interesting

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:02.820
and tasty burger.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:08.460
In 2015, a Bob's Burgers fan named Cole Bowden
started a blog called The Bob's Burgers Experiment.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:13.590
He dutifully recreated dozens of burger-of-the-day
specials based almost entirely on his own

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:18.250
imagination and culinary skills as on the
show, the burgers were little more than brief,

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.260
passing background gags.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:24.220
In 2016, Bowden teamed up with the show's
creative team to release an official cookbook,

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:25.960
The Bob's Burgers Burger Book.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.980
Along with new illustrations and text from
the Belcher family, it includes more than

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:35.330
70 real recipes such as "I Know Why the Cajun
Burger Sings Burger," "Sweaty Palms Burger,"

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.920
and "Shoot-Out at the OK-ra Corral Burger
(comes with fried okra)."

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:43.540
Hollywood is full of great actors, but it
takes a special eye for talent to match performers

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:44.580
to the right part.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.900
Casting is a major part of the undefinable
magic of a great TV series, but the way Bob's

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:53.460
Burgers creator Loren Bouchard assembled his
cast was a little nontraditional and extremely

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.160
optimistic.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:57.560
He wrote every main role with the actor who
would ultimately voice those characters in

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.060
mind.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:07:59.880
Buchard told It's Nice That:

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.930
"I've worked with Jon Benjamin who plays Bob
for over 20 years.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:06.430
I've only ever done shows with Jon in them,
I don't even want to know what it's like to

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:07.560
do a show without him.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.500
I knew I wanted him as the dad and we built
the family around him."

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.670
He thought the female voice John Roberts did
in so many of his videos would work for Linda

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.190
Belcher, and that Kristen Schaal just ought
to be Louise.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:21.260
He's also worked with Gene's Eugene Mirman
before, and wanted to include him in the main

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:21.760
family.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.160
The only cast member not pre-approved: Dan
Mintz, who plays Tina, and was recommended

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:26.980
by Benjamin.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:31.260
Fan art, new art created by fans, inspired
by and starring characters from their favorite

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.460
TV shows, is a wonderful thing.

00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:38.149
But Bob's Burgers producer and artist Tony
Gennaro loves Bob's Burgers just as much as

00:08:38.149 --> 00:08:42.099
viewers do, and makes what might be described
as "fan art" if he wasn't a member of the

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:42.600
show's staff.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:47.519
You see, after each episode's script is written
and printed out for a table read by the cast,

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:50.440
Gennaro makes a special, original title page
illustration.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:54.399
None of these images are based on actual stills
or animation from the episode or any of its

00:08:54.399 --> 00:08:55.399
promotional materials.

00:08:55.399 --> 00:08:59.550
It's just an extra little bit of Bob's Burgers
fun strictly for the people who make Bob's

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:00.100
Burgers.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.340
They often mimic a movie poster, or pay homage
to the well-known posters used to promote

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:05.280
famous movies.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.920
Gennaro's title page for "Uncle Teddy" recalls
the Uncle Buck one-sheet, for example, while

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.960
the "Seaplane!" art is a clear send-up of
Top Gun imagery.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:16.460
Bob's Burgers seamlessly weaves the high with
the low.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.930
Any given episode may present an earnest and
emotionally honest plot about the importance

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:25.350
of family and being a good person...but then
give way to something like Tina Belcher dreaming

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:30.430
about sexy zombies, or a song from Gene Belcher
about the glory of passing gas, for example.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:35.360
This mix of high-minded and low-minded material
is prevalent in an unexpected place: an overlooked

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.180
bit of basic character information.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.500
The last name of the central characters is
Belcher.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:41.840
Why did creator Loren Bouchard stick them
with that?

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.440
He told It's Nice That.

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:47.660
First, the, quote, "silly stupid joke" of
the name appealed to him, but more importantly,

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.980
it was to provide some kind of cultural identification.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:51.160
Bouchard explained:

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:55.380
"My name is French-Canadian and we wanted
them to have some ethnicity but couldn't settle

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:55.880
on what."

00:09:56.020 --> 00:10:00.100
He and the writers selected a name that sounded
at least a little French-Canadian, in addition

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.200
to being a burp joke.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.520
"We're Belchers, from the womb to the tomb!"

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.960
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:09.280
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:13.220
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

